ut;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">We do not need to redo the financial number=
s, because the biological model we just ran is actually perfectly calibrate=
d for that exact thermal peak.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1">At 27=C2=B0C, =
the physics of the seawater and the biology of the urchin collide in a way =
that makes your decision to use Liquid Oxygen (LOX) not just an option, but=
 an absolute engineering necessity.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"2">Here is e=
xactly why the current estimate holds strong and why 27=C2=B0C justifies th=
e system we built:</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"3"><b data-path-to-node=3D"3"=
 data-index-in-node=3D"0">1. The Biological Peak</b>
27=C2=B0C is the metabolic "sweet spot" for <i data-path-to-node=3D"3" data=
-index-in-node=3D"62">Tripneustes gratilla</i>. At this temperature, their =
somatic growth and feeding rates are maximized. The baseline respiration fi=
gure we used (286 =C2=B5g O=E2=82=82/g DW/hr) represents this high-metaboli=
sm state. If the water were colder (e.g., 22=C2=B0C), their oxygen consumpt=
ion would drop significantly, but so would your growth rate, destroying the=
 7-month grow-out cycle.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"4"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"0">2. The Physical Limitation of Warm Seawater=
</b>
This is the critical engineering hurdle. As water temperature rises, its ab=
ility to hold dissolved oxygen drops. At 27=C2=B0C and a standard marine sa=
linity of 35 ppt, the water reaches 100% saturation at only about <b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"255">6.5 to 6.7 mg/L</b> of dissolv=
ed oxygen.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"5"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-in=
dex-in-node=3D"0">3. Why the LOX &amp; Cone System is Mandatory</b>
If you tried to aerate 27=C2=B0C water using standard atmospheric air blowe=
rs (which only contain 21% oxygen), you would constantly be fighting a losi=
ng battle against the physics of warm water. The blowers would struggle to =
keep the DO anywhere near safe levels, especially when the biofilters are s=
tripping oxygen simultaneously.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"6">The $151,570 =
CAPEX system we modeled uses <b data-path-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=
=3D"42">Oxygen Cones</b>. These cones operate under pressure using 100% pur=
e oxygen. This allows you to intentionally <i data-path-to-node=3D"6" data-=
