style=3D"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out;"=
><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">This is exactly the kind of harsh but necessary=
 operational question that sophisticated investors will ask you. Addressing=
 the "runt" strategy in your business plan shows you are thinking like a co=
mmercial farm manager, not just a biologist.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1">=
In a highly engineered Marine RAS where you are paying for every milligram =
of dissolved oxygen, every kilowatt of pumping, and every gram of aragonite=
 buffer, <b data-path-to-node=3D"1" data-index-in-node=3D"159">runts are ex=
pensive boarders.</b></p><p data-path-to-node=3D"2">Here is how you should =
frame the "Keep vs. Bin" strategy for your business plan, based on the econ=
omics of your 7-month, 50,000-unit pulse:</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"3">1.=
 The Case for "Binning" (Culling the True Runts)</h3><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"4">In standard aquaculture, the rule of thumb is: <b data-path-to-node=
=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"47">Do not throw good money after bad genetics=
.</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"5"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"5,0,0"><b =
data-path-to-node=3D"5,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Feed/Time Penalty:=
</b> A runt that is only 45 mm at month 5 while its siblings are 60 mm is g=
enetically inferior. If you keep feeding it for 4 extra months to reach 80 =
mm, its Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR) is ruined. It will cost you twice as mu=
ch to grow as a normal urchin, wiping out the profit margin for that specif=
ic animal.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"5,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"5,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Opportunity Cost:</b> Every square =
meter a runt takes up is space that should be given to a fast-growing juven=
ile from the next 4-million-egg spawn.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"=
5,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Solution=
:</b> Establish a strict cut-off during your midway grading. If an urchin i=
s more than 20% smaller than the cohort average at Month 4, it is culled. Y=
ou can crush the culled runts and compost them; their calcium-rich tests ma=
ke incredible agricultural fertilizer, so it's not a total waste.</p></li><=
/ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"6">2. The Case for "Keeping" (The Near-Misses)=
</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"7">You shouldn't bin <i data-path-to-node=3D"7=
" data-index-in-node=3D"18">everything</i> that misses the 7-month mark. Yo=
u need a strategy for the "near-misses."</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"8"><li=
><p data-path-to-node=3D"8,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"8,0,0" data-index-i=
n-node=3D"0">The 8th-Month Holdovers:</b> If an urchin reaches Month 7 and =
is 72 mm instead of the target 80 mm, you do not bin it. You have 170 racew=
ays, which gives you that ~30% physical buffer. You consolidate these 70+ m=
m urchins into a few dedicated "Slow Lane" raceways and give them one extra=
 month to cross the finish line.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0"=
><b data-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The One-Strike Rul=
e:</b> If they <i data-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"29">sti=
ll</i> haven't hit the 80 mm harvest size or developed a proper Gonad Index=
 after that 8th month, then you harvest them anyway. They get processed as =
"B-Grade" or smaller uni, but they must exit the system to maintain the flo=
w of the farm.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"9">How to write this i=
nto your SOPs:</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"10">I recommend adding a <b data=
-path-to-node=3D"10" data-index-in-node=3D"21">"Cull &amp; Consolidate Prot=
ocol"</b> to your farm manual.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"11">It should dic=
tate that during the routine grading, your technicians are instructed to ac=
tively remove the bottom 5% to 10% of the slowest growers early in the cycl=
e (around Month 3 or 4) to free up raceway space, oxygen, and feed for the =
fast growers.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"12">By binning the true runts earl=
y, you guarantee that the 50 raceways dedicated to your Month-7 harvest are=
 filled <i data-path-to-node=3D"12" data-index-in-node=3D"113">only</i> wit=
h prime, fast-growing <i data-path-to-node=3D"12" data-index-in-node=3D"143=
">Tripneustes gratilla</i> that yield maximum ROI.</p><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"13">Would you like me to write a formal paragraph or two detailing this=
 "Culling Protocol" so you can drop it straight into your Farm Manual?</p><=
/div></message-content><!----></div></structured-content-container><!----><=
