ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">For a =
system moving this much water (450 m=C2=B3/hr), you absolutely want to desi=
gn the UV sterilizer as a <b data-path-to-node=3D"0" data-index-in-node=3D"=
102">Bypass (Side-stream) System</b>, not full flow-through.</p><p data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"1">Here is the engineering logic why a bypass is the standard =
best practice for a commercial Marine RAS of your scale:</p><p data-path-to=
-node=3D"2"><b data-path-to-node=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"0">1. The Phys=
ics of UV Contact Time (The "Kill Rate")</b>
UV sterilization relies entirely on exposure time. If you force the entire =
450 m=C2=B3/hr main flow through a UV array, the water is moving so violent=
ly fast that the pathogens zip right past the bulbs. To get a lethal dose a=
t that velocity, you would need a massive, incredibly expensive commercial =
UV reactor array. By using a bypass, you can pull a smaller percentage of w=
ater (e.g., 20% to 30% of the main flow), slow it down, and guarantee a 99.=
9% pathogen kill rate on that side-stream. Because it=E2=80=99s a recircula=
ting system, that continuous "polishing" keeps the overall pathogen load in=
 the whole farm practically at zero.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"3"><b data-=
path-to-node=3D"3" data-index-in-node=3D"0">2. Hydraulic Backpressure (Head=
 Loss)</b>
Forcing your entire main flow through the tight quartz sleeves of a UV bank=
 creates massive backpressure. Your 22kW pumps would have to work significa=
ntly harder, burning through your electricity OPEX just to fight the bottle=
neck.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"4"><b data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-index-i=
n-node=3D"0">3. Zero-Downtime Maintenance</b>
UV quartz sleeves get dirty and bulbs burn out (usually around 9,000 hours)=
. If your UV is on a bypass loop, your technicians can simply close two iso=
lation valves, drain the UV vessel, and clean it without ever stopping the =
main life-support flow to your 170 raceways. If it were flow-through, you w=
ould have to shut down the main pumps to service the UV, putting the urchin=
s at risk.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"5"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-in=
dex-in-node=3D"0">How to integrate it:</b>
Just like we designed a "High-Flow Bypass Manifold" for your Titanium Heat =
Pumps (costed at $14,200 in the thermal estimate), the UV system will sit o=
n a parallel branch.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"6">The clean water exits th=
e MBBR, the main pumps push it up the trunk line, and a valve bleeds off ab=
out 100 m=C2=B3/hr into the UV/Chiller loops before it all recombines and g=
ravity-feeds out to the raceways.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"7">Does that m=
ake sense for the plumbing layout you are envisioning, or were you thinking=
 of using an open-channel gravity UV system?</p></div></message-content><!-=
